CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The mechanism is explicit, but its observable effect depends on live execution-context initialization, the database response to CURRENT_CATALOG(), existing bootstrap settings, and whether catalog-setting operations succeed.

Databricks now uses a specialized execution context that tracks the session's active catalog, discovers it through CURRENT_CATALOG(), can seed an empty bootstrap default, and propagates a source context's default catalog into newly initialized contexts.

Likely behavioral changes
  • New Databricks execution contexts use catalog-aware behavior instead of the generic execution-context implementation, allowing their reported default catalog to be resolved from a separately tracked active catalog name.
  • When an initial Databricks context is initialized, it refreshes generic defaults and queries CURRENT_CATALOG(); a nonempty result becomes the context's active catalog and also fills the bootstrap default catalog when that setting is empty.
  • A Databricks context initialized from another context attempts to adopt that context's default catalog, so related contexts can begin in the same catalog rather than independently discovering a different default.

Investigate
  • Trace the execution-context lifecycle to establish when createExecutionContext and initializeContextState run, and which context types can be supplied as initFrom.
  • Find consumers of the default catalog and determine whether metadata browsing, SQL execution, object qualification, or UI selection relies on this context value.
  • Examine generic superclass and Databricks JDBC behavior around catalog switching, default refresh, bootstrap settings, and CURRENT_CATALOG() support.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not establish which framework callers invoke context creation and initialization, or whether every Databricks connection follows this lifecycle.
  • It is not shown which catalog-sensitive operations consume getDefaultCatalog or the bootstrap default after this change.
  • Whether CURRENT_CATALOG() succeeds, returns a nonempty value, and differs from the tracked value depends on the live driver and session.
  • The actual session catalog and whether a deployment already has a configured bootstrap default cannot be determined from the diff.
  • The superclass behavior for refreshing and setting defaults, including whether setDefaultCatalog can partially alter session state before throwing, is not supplied.

